1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,898
<font color="#000000">DeepJoint is a robust survival model for clinical data.</font>

2
00:00:03,978 --> 00:00:08,199
<font color="#000000">In medicine, when and which tests get ordered is
itself informative,</font>

3
00:00:08,279 --> 00:00:10,792
<font color="#000000">reflecting how clinicians treat patients,</font>

4
00:00:10,872 --> 00:00:13,828
<font color="#000000">a phenomenon the authors call clinical presence.</font>

5
00:00:13,908 --> 00:00:17,749
<font color="#000000">When these patterns shift, standard prediction
models degrade.</font>

6
00:00:17,829 --> 00:00:24,832
<font color="#000000">DeepJoint is a multi-task recurrent network that
models three clinical presence processes jointly with survival,</font>

7
00:00:24,912 --> 00:00:28,690
<font color="#000000">delivering both strong discrimination and greater
robustness.</font>

8
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:35,279
<font color="#000000">Observational medical data arise from the
interaction between patients and the healthcare system.</font>

9
00:00:35,359 --> 00:00:37,289
<font color="#000000">When a clinician orders a test,</font>

10
00:00:37,369 --> 00:00:41,503
<font color="#000000">its timing and its existence carry information
about the patient.</font>

11
00:00:41,583 --> 00:00:45,523
<font color="#000000">Most models ignore this, assuming sampling is
non-informative.</font>

12
00:00:45,603 --> 00:00:50,191
<font color="#000000">Ignoring clinical presence yields sub-optimal,
non-transportable models;</font>

13
00:00:50,271 --> 00:00:52,460
<font color="#000000">modelling it explicitly is the fix.</font>

14
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:58,363
<font color="#000000">A key challenge is heterogeneity: the same patient
population can appear very differently</font>

15
00:00:58,443 --> 00:01:00,692
<font color="#000000">depending on the observation process,</font>

16
00:01:00,772 --> 00:01:03,966
<font color="#000000">and that process shifts across countries, over time,</font>

17
00:01:04,046 --> 00:01:06,421
<font color="#000000">and even between weekdays and weekends.</font>

18
00:01:06,501 --> 00:01:09,757
<font color="#000000">The literature has studied covariate and label shift,</font>

19
00:01:09,837 --> 00:01:13,661
<font color="#000000">but shift in the observation process itself is
under-explored.</font>

20
00:01:13,741 --> 00:01:19,830
<font color="#000000">DeepJoint asks how explicitly modelling clinical
presence makes survival models more robust to it.</font>

21
00:01:20,210 --> 00:01:26,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper contributes a deep joint model that
treats clinical presence as multi-task learning.</font>

22
00:01:26,144 --> 00:01:29,852
<font color="#FFFFFF">A shared recurrent embedding feeds four heads:
longitudinal,</font>

23
00:01:29,932 --> 00:01:33,135
<font color="#FFFFFF">inter-observation timing, missingness, and survival,</font>

24
00:01:33,215 --> 00:01:38,185
<font color="#FFFFFF">all trained together by maximising a combined
likelihood with dynamic weighting.</font>

25
00:01:38,265 --> 00:01:42,478
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a representation that encodes the
observation process,</font>

26
00:01:42,558 --> 00:01:46,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">giving both a predictive edge and robustness when
that process changes.</font>

27
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:54,234
<font color="#000000">A Long Short Term Memory network extracts an
embedding from each patient's irregular sequence of laboratory tests.</font>

28
00:01:54,314 --> 00:01:59,617
<font color="#000000">This embedding drives three clinical-presence
heads: a longitudinal head predicting next</font>

29
00:01:59,697 --> 00:02:02,064
<font color="#000000">test values under a Gaussian likelihood,</font>

30
00:02:02,144 --> 00:02:06,835
<font color="#000000">a missingness head predicting which tests appear
under a Bernoulli likelihood,</font>

31
00:02:06,915 --> 00:02:10,506
<font color="#000000">and a timing head modelling the inter-observation
intensity.</font>

32
00:02:10,586 --> 00:02:14,360
<font color="#000000">A DeepSurv head models survival under Cox
proportional hazards.</font>

33
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:17,418
<font color="#000000">The four losses are combined by dynamic weighting,</font>

34
00:02:17,498 --> 00:02:21,150
<font color="#000000">balanced by a hyperparameter alpha, and optimised
end to end.</font>

35
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:23,363
<font color="#000000">Experiments use MIMIC-three,</font>

36
00:02:23,443 --> 00:02:29,374
<font color="#000000">an intensive-care database of anonymised laboratory
tests for over thirty-eight thousand</font>

37
00:02:29,454 --> 00:02:33,200
<font color="#000000">patients at Beth Israel Deaconess between 2001 and 2012.</font>

38
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:39,621
<font color="#000000">Restricting to those surviving the first
twenty-four hours leaves a cohort of 30,834 patients.</font>

39
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:46,247
<font color="#000000">Models predict in-hospital survival from the
embedding at the last observation in that first day,</font>

40
00:02:46,327 --> 00:02:51,370
<font color="#000000">compared by time-dependent concordance index and
Brier score at one, seven,</font>

41
00:02:51,450 --> 00:02:52,600
<font color="#000000">and fourteen days.</font>

42
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:54,948
<font color="#000000">On a random population split,</font>

43
00:02:55,028 --> 00:03:02,506
<font color="#000000">the three proposed methods deliver
competitive-to-best discrimination against models using the same inputs.</font>

44
00:03:02,586 --> 00:03:06,533
<font color="#000000">Strikingly, DeepJoint, which sees only laboratory values,</font>

45
00:03:06,613 --> 00:03:11,760
<font color="#000000">already outperforms both an LSTM that ignores
clinical presence and GRU-D,</font>

46
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:14,303
<font color="#000000">which consumes missingness as input.</font>

47
00:03:14,383 --> 00:03:18,894
<font color="#000000">So modelling the observation process, even without
feeding it in,</font>

48
00:03:18,974 --> 00:03:21,366
<font color="#000000">yields a more predictive embedding.</font>

49
00:03:21,446 --> 00:03:26,240
<font color="#000000">Fine-tuning adds a further edge, reaching a one-day
C-index of 0.878.</font>

50
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:30,712
<font color="#000000">The approach decomposes into three variants against
six baselines.</font>

51
00:03:30,792 --> 00:03:35,453
<font color="#000000">DeepJointFeature, which adds clinical-presence
features to the joint model,</font>

52
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:39,751
<font color="#000000">improves over plain DeepJoint and matches a strong
feature baseline.</font>

53
00:03:39,831 --> 00:03:45,251
<font color="#000000">The fine-tuned variant reaches the highest
population-level discrimination but overfits</font>

54
00:03:45,331 --> 00:03:47,526
<font color="#000000">when the observation process shifts.</font>

55
00:03:47,606 --> 00:03:49,612
<font color="#000000">Across the robustness experiment,</font>

56
00:03:49,692 --> 00:03:54,922
<font color="#000000">DeepJointFeature best combines strong
discrimination with proximity to the diagonal,</font>

57
00:03:55,002 --> 00:03:57,640
<font color="#000000">transferring most reliably across settings.</font>

58
00:03:58,020 --> 00:04:03,735
<font color="#000000">The headline numbers: a concordance index of 0.878
at the one-day horizon,</font>

59
00:04:03,815 --> 00:04:07,102
<font color="#000000">on a cohort of 30,834 MIMIC-three patients.</font>

60
00:04:07,182 --> 00:04:13,288
<font color="#000000">DeepJoint jointly captures three dimensions of
clinical presence, longitudinal,</font>

61
00:04:13,368 --> 00:04:19,161
<font color="#000000">timing, and missingness, alongside survival, across
horizons of one, seven,</font>

62
00:04:19,241 --> 00:04:20,570
<font color="#000000">and fourteen days.</font>

63
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:25,180
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: the way clinical data are sampled is
itself informative,</font>

64
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:30,475
<font color="#000000">and jointly modelling that observation process with
survival produces predictions that</font>

65
00:04:30,555 --> 00:04:35,339
<font color="#000000">are both more accurate and markedly more robust
when clinical practice changes.</font>

66
00:04:35,419 --> 00:04:37,740
<font color="#000000">Clinical presence is signal, not noise,</font>

67
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:41,250
<font color="#000000">and modelling it makes medical predictions transportable.</font>
